ometimes be risky if they don't secure the corner immediately, potentially allowing the opponent to leverage the remaining empty edge squares.\n- **(D, 1)**: This extends my influence from the A1 corner along the top edge. It flips D2. However, it leaves a gap (B1, C1) which might be complicated to manage, and it flips fewer pieces than C6.\n\nReason: I choose **(C, 6)** because it maximizes the number of flipped pieces in the center (stability and material gain) and restricts the opponent's future mobility by removing their central anchors. It is a safe, consolidating move that does not expose me to edge traps or give away corner access.\n\nAnswer: (C, 6)"}
